  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Promoting Thermophilic Anaerobic Digestion in Faecal Sludge Treatment to Contribute to the Prevention of Cholera Outbreaks in Bangladesh&#x2019;s Rohingyas Refugee Camps</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IOM considers research uptake and dissemination a critical step of the project cycle. Effective research uptake ensures that the project findings will be used by policymakers and Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) practitioners to inform future policy and Faecal Sludge Management (FSM) practices and strategies in Bangladesh. IOM is developing standard operating procedures and the final report to be disseminated through the internal IOM WASH Community of Practice (CoP) and webpage. Externally, IOM ensures to connect with well-known platforms dedicated to support knowledge and improve practices on the Emergency FSM discourse. Another venue for dissemination is the national WASH sector, and the FSM Technical Working Group (TWiG) operating under the umbrella of the Global WASH Cluster (GWC), of which IOM has been a member since 2019. Other activities include the regular engagement of key stakeholders through tailored community awareness activities. Advocacy efforts through various means address and target key stakeholders, donors and the broader community of WASH experts. Lastly, to foster the use of evidence compiled during the project by other WASH practitioners and actors, IOM ensures a robust monitoring and evaluation of the activities to create evidence-based results, demonstrate impact and effectiveness of the intervention, and trigger learning processes.</narrative>
  </description>
